\import{fnip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fnip}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{An annulus that excludes the focus}
\date{2026-11-26}

\p{A compact region in the plane that contains no
[equilibrium](fnip-0014), and that the flow does not leave, contains
a [closed orbit](fnip-0052). The forward orbit of any state in the
region stays in the region, so it has an accumulation point. The
[omega-limit set](fnip-0052) of that state is a nonempty compact
subset of the region and contains no equilibrium, and
\ref{fnip-0054} says that this set is a closed orbit.}

\p{\citet{Exercise 1.8.2, §1.8, p. 44}{guckenheimer1983nonlinear}
applies the theorem to two nested closed curves. The flow crosses the
outer curve inward and the inner curve outward, and the van der Pol
oscillator then has a closed orbit in the annulus. The same page
treats a uniqueness argument as a further step.}

\p{For the Morris–Lecar velocity of \ref{fnip-0005} at
#{I_{\mathrm{app}}=100}, take the rectangle #{R} given by
#{V\in[-70,80]} and #{n\in[0,1]}. On #{n=0},
#{\dot n=\phi\, n_{\infty}(V)/\tau_{n}(V)}. For every finite #{V}
one has #{0<n_{\infty}(V)<1}, because #{\tanh} takes values in
#{(-1,1)}, and #{\tau_{n}(V)>0}, so #{\dot n>0}. On #{n=1},
#{\dot n=\phi\,(n_{\infty}(V)-1)/\tau_{n}(V)<0}. On the side
#{V=80}, with #{n\in[0,1]},
##{C_{\mathrm{M}}\dot V=100-280-1312n+176\,m_{\infty}(80).}
Since #{n\ge 0} and #{m_{\infty}(80)\le 1}, the right-hand side is at
most #{-4}, and #{\dot V\le -0.2}. On the side #{V=-70},
##{C_{\mathrm{M}}\dot V=120-112n+836\,m_{\infty}(-70).}
Since #{n\le 1} and #{m_{\infty}(-70)\ge 0}, the right-hand side is at
least #{8}, and #{\dot V\ge 0.4}. The velocity points into #{R} along
the boundary of #{R}.}

\p{The inner boundary is the curve
##{(V,n)=(V_{*},n_{*})+(0.6324u+0.7804w,\ 0.004u),
   \qquad (u,w)=(\cos\theta,\sin\theta).}
Its center is the equilibrium #{(V_{*},n_{*})} of \ref{fnip-0017}.
Solving for the velocities of #{u} and #{w}, the radial component
#{u\dot u+w\dot w} is at least #{0.0076} on 2880 equally spaced
angles. On this curve the velocity points out of the elliptical
disk, and the equilibrium lies in the open disk. The closed region
between the curve and the boundary of #{R} is compact, the velocity
points into it, and the equilibrium of \ref{fnip-0017} lies outside
it, so the region contains a closed orbit.}

\p{A disk whose only equilibrium is this unstable focus yields the same
conclusion. The eigenvalues of \ref{fnip-0037} have positive real
part, and the homeomorphism of \ref{fnip-0043} carries that local
departure to the nonlinear flow, so the focus lies in the omega-limit
set of no other state. The omega-limit set of any other state in the
disk then contains no equilibrium. The region drawn for this current
is the annulus of the exercise.}

\p{If the region contains an equilibrium, the omega-limit set may be a
closed orbit, or a union of equilibria and orbits that connect them.
\citet{§1.8, p. 45}{guckenheimer1983nonlinear} records these
possibilities for a planar flow. The argument uses the plane.}
